\section*{Finiteness of the little finitistic dimension}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a counterexample in the selected manuscript. A single finite-dimensional complex algebra is claimed to have finite modules whose finite projective dimensions are unbounded.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
One fixed finitely presented algebra encodes arbitrarily long terminating processes through a twisted derived tensor functor. The subsequent finite-dimensional realization converts these tensor histories into ordinary projective resolutions, retaining unbounded termination lengths without changing the final algebra.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
One finite complex algebra supports modules with finite projective dimension at least $2m-2$ for arbitrarily large integers $m$. Consequently its little finitistic dimension is infinite, giving the requested counterexample within Artin algebras. This is presented as a claimed negative answer.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 198, the Lean scope includes unbounded finite projective dimensions over one complex algebra and a separate extreme left/right finitistic and injective-generation asymmetry. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{FinitisticAsymmetry.lean}, \texttt{LittleFinitistic.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.LittleFinitistic.Main.exists\_counterexample}, \texttt{OAI.LittleFinitistic.extreme\_left\_right\_asymmetry}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

